{"id":"quetzal","name":"Resplendent Quetzal","sci":"Pharomachrus mocinno","body":"kingfisher","collection":"Neotropics","tags":{"features":["whistler","cackler","display flier"],"region":["Central America","Southern Mexico"]},"params":{"beak":0.49,"beak_track":0.674,"breath":0.09,"closure":0.136,"coupling":0.152,"echo":0.597,"mass":0.0,"mode":0.0,"mouth":0.261,"mouth_q":0.523,"oec":8580.462,"oec_depth":0.755,"oec_track":0.242,"pressure":1.047,"pulse":0.011,"speed":1.0,"sweep":1.0,"tension":0.174,"tongue_h":0.523,"tongue_p":0.488,"trachea":6.792,"turb":0.188,"two":0.0,"two_int":4.977,"volume":0.8},"scale":[51,59,71,81,84,115],"match":{"evals":580,"loud_db":0.1,"pitch_cents":8.0,"spec_db":5.1,"total":80.5},"look":{"body":[4.0,2.6,2.4],"head":1.55,"neck":0.7,"tail":2.6,"beak":{"len":0.9,"depth":0.6,"curve":0.25,"hook":0.08},"colors":{"body":"#1f8a3b","belly":"#c8102e","head":"#24913f","wing":"#1c7a4a","tail":"#f2f2f2","beak":"#e8c230","beak2":"#d9b229","eye":"#2b1a10","legs":"#6b6250"},"crest":{"len":1.1,"back":0.15,"w":1.0,"color":"#2fa34a"},"streamers":{"len":9.0,"w":0.3,"color":"#2e9a44"},"accents":[{"body":true,"at":[-1.2,0.5,0],"r":[2.4,0.9,1.25],"rot":-0.35,"color":"#151515","layer":0}]},"model_note":"Nobody has studied a trogon's syrinx yet. The one trogon checked in a large survey of closed-mouth calling (Riede et al. 2016) called with its mouth open, so Carol does not use the closed-beak dove model here, even though it scored slightly higher (82% against 81%). Instead the quetzal uses the general non-songbird model: two coupled membranes where the windpipe meets the bronchi (the same model as the kookaburra). Its song notes are mellow downward slurs at about 0.75-1.1 kHz.","image":"img/quetzal.jpg","image_caption":"John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 2nd ed., part III: Pharomacrus mocinno (Quezal)","plates":[{"image":"img/quetzal.jpg","caption":"John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 2nd ed., part III: Pharomacrus mocinno (Quezal)","artist":"John Gould","year":"1858","credit":"John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 2nd ed., part III: Pharomacrus mocinno (Quezal). J. Gould & W. Hart del et lith. Harvard University, Museum of Comparative Zoology, Ernst Mayr Library copy, digitised for the Biodiversity Heritage Library","url":"https://archive.org/details/monographtrogon3goul/page/n3/mode/1up","bhl":"https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/part/323638","licence":"Public domain"},{"image":"img/quetzal/gould-1835-38.jpg","caption":"John Gould and Elizabeth Gould, John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 1st ed., vol. single volume (1st edition), pl. 21: Trogon resplendens (Resplendent Trogon)","artist":"John Gould and Elizabeth Gould","year":"1835","credit":"John Gould and Elizabeth Gould, John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 1st ed., vol. single volume (1st edition), pl. 21: Trogon resplendens (Resplendent Trogon). Drawn from Nature & on Stone by J. & E. Gould. Smithsonian Libraries copy, digitised for the Biodiversity Heritage Library (Internet Archive monographtrogon00goul)","url":"https://archive.org/details/monographtrogon00goul/page/n103/mode/1up","bhl":"https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/58474132","licence":"Public domain"}],"taxonomy":{"order":"Trogoniformes","family":"Trogonidae","genus":"Pharomachrus","species":"mocinno","authority":"de la Llave, 1832","original":"Pharomachrus mocinno de la Llave, 1832","ioc_url":"https://www.worldbirdnames.org/new/ioc-lists/master-list-2/","gbif_url":"https://www.gbif.org/species/2496723","list":"IOC World Bird List","plate_name":"Pharomacrus mocinno","plate_common":"Quezal","plate_artist":"John Gould"},"info":{"family":"Trogonidae (trogons and quetzals)","overview":"The resplendent quetzal is the biggest of the trogons, a shimmering green bird of the misty mountain cloud forests of southern Mexico and Central America. A breeding male has a scarlet belly, a fuzzy green crest and a pair of green streamers trailing far beyond his tail, often twice his body length. Quetzals sit quietly in the canopy most of the time, but in the breeding season males sing a slow, mellow series of whistled 'kyow' notes and show off in swooping display flights. The quetzal was sacred to the Maya and Aztecs and is Guatemala's national bird.","deep_dive":{"habitat":"Wet, mossy montane forest, especially cloud forest, mostly between about 900 and 3,200 m. Quetzals live in the canopy and subcanopy and seldom come to the ground. They also use forest edges and clearings with scattered fruiting trees, and move down to lower, wetter forest outside the breeding season.","range":"The highlands from southern Mexico (Oaxaca and Chiapas) through Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras and Nicaragua to Costa Rica and western Panama (Chiriqui). There are two subspecies: mocinno in the north and the slightly smaller costaricensis in Costa Rica and Panama, separated by the lowlands of the Nicaraguan depression.","behaviour":"Usually quiet and solitary, sitting still for long periods. Quetzals are fruit specialists, especially wild avocados and other fruits of the laurel family. They pluck fruit in a short hovering sally, swallow it whole and later bring up the stone, which spreads the trees through the forest. They also take insects, frogs, lizards and snails, especially for the chicks. They follow the fruiting of their food trees, moving up and down the mountains with the seasons. Pairs dig or enlarge a hole in a rotten tree trunk, and both parents incubate, the male mostly by day and the female at night. Courting males rise above the canopy and then swoop down with the streamers rippling behind them.","voice":"Quetzals are noisiest in the breeding season. The male's song is a slow series of full, mellow, slurred whistles, often doubled, written as 'kyow', 'keow' or 'kowee', repeated with pauses. In the recordings collected here the song notes sit mostly around 0.75-1.1 kHz, each note slurring downwards. Other calls include harsher cackling and chattering notes ('wac-wac', 'wec-wec') given in flight or when the bird is excited or alarmed, soft whistles and buzzy notes. Researchers in Guatemala sorted the repertoire into two long-range calls used for territory and contact and two short-range calls, and found that the songs of the two subspecies differ clearly.","anatomy":"A non-songbird of the order Trogoniformes. Like other birds it makes sound with a syrinx where the windpipe divides into the two bronchi; no study of the quetzal's or any trogon's syrinx was found, so details of its sound source are unknown. It is also not known whether quetzals sing with the bill closed and the throat puffed out (as doves do): a 2016 survey of closed-mouth calling in birds coded the one trogon it checked (red-headed trogon) as an open-mouth singer. Trogons have a foot found in no other birds, with the first and second toes pointing backwards (heterodactyl), very short weak legs, and a short, strong bill with a wide gape; the male quetzal's bill is yellow and partly hidden under green bristly feathers. The long 'tail' streamers are actually the upper tail coverts, which grow over the real tail. The green colour is structural (light scattering on feathers that contain brown melanin), so it shifts from gold-green to blue in different light."}},"sources":{"recordings":[{"id":"XC1188164","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/1188164","recordist":"Alain Verneau","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","location":"Copey (near Copey District), Dota, San Jos\u00e9 Province","date":"2026-03-03","used":false,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC1188163","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/1188163","recordist":"Alain Verneau","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","location":"Copey (near Copey District), Dota, San Jos\u00e9 Province","date":"2026-03-03","used":false,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC774050","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/774050","recordist":"Paul Driver","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","location":"Monte Verde Cloud Forest Reserve","date":"2013-05-30","used":true,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC137766","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/137766","recordist":"Paul Driver","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 3.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/","location":"Monte Verde Cloud Forest Reserve","date":"2013-05-30","used":true,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC872148","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/872148","recordist":"Leander Khil","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","location":"Santiago Atitl\u00e1n, Santiago Atitl\u00e1n, Solol\u00e1 Department","date":"2024-02-09","used":true,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC326653","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/326653","recordist":"Beatrix Saadi-Varchmin","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","location":"near Paraiso Lodge, Parque Nacional Los Quetzales (near San Gerardo de Dota), Dota, Cartago","date":"2015-02-18","used":true,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC332262","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/332262","recordist":"Bobby Wilcox","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","location":"Reserva Curi-Cancha, Monteverde","date":"2016-07-29","used":false,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC133593","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/133593","recordist":"Thore Noernberg","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 3.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/","location":"Boquete, Boquete, Chiriqui","date":"2013-04-30","used":false,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC498299","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/498299","recordist":"Odirlei Vieira da Fonseca","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 4.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/","location":"La Paz, San Ram\u00f3n, Provincia de Alajuela","date":"2019-08-02","used":false,"source":"xeno-canto"},{"id":"XC171051","url":"https://xeno-canto.org/171051","recordist":"Pablo Bola\u00f1os Sittler","licence":"CC BY-NC-SA 3.0","licence_url":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/","location":"Biotopo del Quetzal","date":"2013-06-09","used":false,"source":"xeno-canto"}],"image":{"credit":"John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 2nd ed., part III: Pharomacrus mocinno (Quezal). J. Gould & W. Hart del et lith. Harvard University, Museum of Comparative Zoology, Ernst Mayr Library copy, digitised for the Biodiversity Heritage Library","url":"https://archive.org/details/monographtrogon3goul/page/n3/mode/1up","bhl":"https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/part/323638","source":"Scan","licence":"Public domain"},"plates":[{"image":"img/quetzal.jpg","caption":"John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 2nd ed., part III: Pharomacrus mocinno (Quezal)","artist":"John Gould","year":"1858","credit":"John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 2nd ed., part III: Pharomacrus mocinno (Quezal). J. Gould & W. Hart del et lith. Harvard University, Museum of Comparative Zoology, Ernst Mayr Library copy, digitised for the Biodiversity Heritage Library","url":"https://archive.org/details/monographtrogon3goul/page/n3/mode/1up","bhl":"https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/part/323638","licence":"Public domain"},{"image":"img/quetzal/gould-1835-38.jpg","caption":"John Gould and Elizabeth Gould, John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 1st ed., vol. single volume (1st edition), pl. 21: Trogon resplendens (Resplendent Trogon)","artist":"John Gould and Elizabeth Gould","year":"1835","credit":"John Gould and Elizabeth Gould, John Gould, A Monograph of the Trogonidae, or Family of Trogons, 1st ed., vol. single volume (1st edition), pl. 21: Trogon resplendens (Resplendent Trogon). Drawn from Nat